From: Noa Himesaka <himesaka@noa.codes>
Subject: [PATCH] firmware: qcom: add a userspace interface to QSEE trusted applications

Some phones ship vendor trusted applications that their Android stack
loads from the HLOS side through downstream qseecom and drives with
QSEECom_send_cmd(). Samsung's fingerprint TA (securefp) is one: the
sensor sits on a TrustZone-owned SPI bus, and only the TA can talk to it.

Add /dev/qsee-app on top of the QSEECOM calls from 0040. A descriptor
loads one application (QSEE_APP_IOC_LOAD: look it up, otherwise load its
.mdt/.bNN image with APP_START, and allocate the shared buffer the
commands live in) and sends it commands (QSEE_APP_IOC_SEND: request at
the start of the shared buffer, response at the 64-byte aligned offset
after it, as the Android client library lays them out). An application
the descriptor loaded is shut down on close.

Both areas go in and come back, because apps use the shared buffer the
way QSEECom clients see it: securefp reads an output capacity from the
response area, and Samsung's skeymast answers inside the request area.
Images may be ELF64 (securefp, authhat) or ELF32 (hwvault, skeymast).

QSEE_APP_IOC_VMBUF gives the descriptor one buffer owned by another VM
(qcom_scm_assign_mem from HLOS to the requested VMID, read/write) and
reports its physical address: keymaster64's SPU_INIT takes such a buffer,
owned by CP_SPSS_SP_SHARED (0x22), as stock's ION sp_mem heap provides
it. It is assigned back and freed when the descriptor closes, unless
QSEE_APP_VMBUF_KEEP asks for it to stay with the other VM until reboot
(keymaster64 keeps using its SPU buffer after SPU_INIT returns). The
buffer is allocated below 4 GiB, as the secure processor needs.

QSEE_APP_VMBUF_HLOS keeps HLOS in the destination list, so the buffer is
shared rather than handed over: {VMID read/write, HLOS read/write}, from
dma_alloc_coherent() with a 32-bit mask, and mmap() maps it. The SPSS's
NVM service needs that: sec_nvm loads each partition into a buffer owned
by CP_SPSS_HLOS_SHARED (0x24) and HLOS, as the ION sp_mem heap gives it,
and commits from it; its scratch buffers are the same kind.

Listener-blocked and INCOMPLETE results are still not handled.

Signed-off-by: Noa Himesaka <himesaka@noa.codes>
Assisted-by: Claude:claude-opus-5-5
---
 drivers/firmware/qcom/Kconfig         |   12 +
 drivers/firmware/qcom/Makefile        |    1 
 drivers/firmware/qcom/qcom_qsee_app.c |  466 +++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++
 include/uapi/linux/qcom_qsee_app.h    |   85 ++++++
 4 files changed, 564 insertions(+)

